  "challenges": [
    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_sdm_211",
      "name": "crud-safety-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Which rows does this statement really touch?",
      "description": "Two pieces of evidence from the CodeDev admin tool: a search query built by string concatenation, and a learner count after the team switched from hard delete to soft delete. Decide whether each statement is true or false.",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "outcomes": ["queries"],
      "points": 10,
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The old admin tool searched learners by email like this (Python, sqlite3):\n\nemail = form[\"email\"]\nsql = \"SELECT user_id, email, name FROM users WHERE email = '\" + email + \"'\"\nrows = db.execute(sql).fetchall()\n\nSomeone types this into the search box:\n\nx' OR '1'='1\n\nStatement: \"The query returns every row of users, because the input closes the string literal and adds a condition that is true for every row.\"",
          "explanation": "True. After concatenation the database receives WHERE email = 'x' OR '1'='1'. The first quote of the input ends the literal, and '1'='1' is true for every row, so the OR makes the whole condition true. The database cannot tell which part of the text was meant as data. With a bound parameter - WHERE email = :email and {\"email\": email} - the value travels separately from the SQL text and is compared as one plain string, so the same input matches no row. A common misconception is that escaping quotes by hand, or \"only staff use this form\", is enough; parameters are the fix, and they also cover every input you did not think of.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The team switched cancellation from a hard delete to a soft delete. Before:\n\nDELETE FROM enrollments WHERE user_id = :user_id AND course_id = :course_id;\n\nAfter:\n\nUPDATE enrollments SET status = 'cancelled'\nWHERE user_id = :user_id AND course_id = :course_id AND status = 'active';\n\nThe course page was not changed. It still counts learners with:\n\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM enrollments WHERE course_id = :course_id;\n\nStatement: \"After the switch, this query still returns the number of learners currently enrolled in the course.\"",
          "explanation": "False. With the hard delete, a cancelled enrollment disappeared, so COUNT(*) only saw current learners. With the soft delete the row stays in the table with status 'cancelled', and this query counts it. Every read that means \"current\" must now add AND status <> 'cancelled' (or read from a view that does). That is the price of soft delete: the history is kept, but each query has to remember to hide it. Verify by running the count before and after cancelling one test enrollment: it should go down by one.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "True",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "False",
              "correct": true
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
